From b12ba67b28f24ec48794664adf943288e2c24d1a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 21:56:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] kanban: close kept board handles once the board file
 is removed

A removed board is never read again, so every kept-handle cache that
revalidates a path only on its next read pinned the deleted kanban.db,
-wal and -shm until the process exited. hermes-hub 2026-11-13: the probe
board auw-probe-t01a1d6ae was removed at 21:06:46 and the askeve-builder
and askeve-reviewer gateways still held fds on its deleted files 30+ min
later (stranded_sqlite_watch filed t_01a1d6bf4dc0c0e0 and
t_01a1d6bfa283d189). The reviewer also held a read-write main-db fd: the
shared cached_ro reader falls back to mode=rw on a fresh board whose WAL
sidecars do not exist yet.

Holders on main, each now with a release_gone() that closes only idle
handles whose path no longer exists (FileNotFoundError/NotADirectoryError;
EACCES keeps the handle), never one under its borrower:
- sync loop BoardWatch (_WATCH), moved to kanban_sync_board_watch.py so
  kanban_sync_client stays under its FILE_LINES cap;
- kanban_session_activity._RO_CACHE (cached_ro), in kanban_reader_retention;
- kanban_kept_reader per-thread connections (any thread);
- sqlite_safe_read pread fds (release_unlinked_fds).

kanban_gone_board_handles.sweep() runs them all (skipping caches whose
module was never imported) from the sync loop right after board_paths()
every round, and from gateway housekeeping every tick, so gateways that do
not run the sync loop are covered too.

Proof: tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_gone_board_handles.py (13 tests; 15 mutants of
the new clauses all killed, including the borrow-race rechecks).
RED on main: the loop test (query through _WATCH, rmtree the board dir,
one more loop pass) found 4 descriptors on the deleted board files
(kanban.db (deleted), -wal, -shm ...); GREEN with this change.

Task: t_01a1d6d62dab2970
